  abstract:'Chronic pain (CP) carries social and affective consequences that are difficult to quantify in rodent models. Ultrasonic vocalizations (USVs) have been proposed as a proxy of affective state and a coordinator of social interaction. Here we characterized USVs emitted during a three-chamber (3-CH) social paradigm in spared nerve injury (SNI), SHAM and naive Wistar-Han male and female rats, recorded across sociability and social-preference sessions. Calls were identified with DeepSqueak using a 50-kHz custom-trained detector network (RD-V2), selected for its nearest precision and accurate call-contour delimitation. USV parameters (number of calls, call length, principal frequency, bandwidth and sinuosity) were compared by three-way ANOVA across sex, target group and explorer group, with explorer treated as a repeated measure. In the sociability session, the presence of a social partner (Naive vs. Empty) markedly increased the number of calls and their bandwidth, with no significant effect on call length, principal frequency or sinuosity. In the social-preference session, the SNI target side elicited more calls overall, while sex differences and SNI-versus-SHAM differences in call structure (call length, delta frequency, and sinuosity) were male-specific — males emitted longer, broader-bandwidth and more sinuous calls. These findings suggest that USV acoustic structure is sensitive to immediate social context and that pain-associated vocal differences may be sex-dependent, though results are limited by the spatial pooling of calls within chambers, exclusion of 22-kHz detections owing to a high proportion of acoustic artefacts, and the stratified sampling used for network training.',
  keywords:['Ultrasonic vocalizations','chronic pain','spared nerve injury','three-chamber paradigm','DeepSqueak','social behavior'],
